{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KQLx15YBaWVmdGe5eGqEoA","type":"tool_result","content":"8:## There was a second tree here until US-072\n18:## There were two trees here (US-052)\n67:## Rules every module follows\n112:## `cast.js` is the surface for work that has not happened (US-075)\n143:## `ipc-engine.js` is not an IPC module any more (US-044)\n177:  `null` endpoint is a wait rather than a failure -- the host already\n185:## Implementation pattern (set by `ipc-os.js`, US-020)\n222:## Gotchas\n  therefore has the host send the whole list as an array and the Tauri\n  shim spread it, so both trees hand the caller the same\n  `(first, ...rest)`. The parity test only checks arity, so a shim\n  that forgets to spread passes it and hands the caller one array.\n- The two **request/response exports** (`checkForExternalPlayer`,\n  `getEngineEndpoint`) were a `send` plus an `ipcRenderer.once` on the\n  same channel under Electron, which is why they read as the odd ones\n  out. Under Tauri they are ordinary `invoke` calls that resolve with\n  what the command returned -- the pair collapsed into the promise the\n  callers already had. Both still *wait on the host*, though, and\n  their commands are `async` for that reason (US-070, US-080): the\n  engine one waits out a sidecar restart, and the external-player one\n  probes every place VLC might be, a process each. A synchronous\n  command would do either on the main thread with the window frozen\n  behind it.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d368e2bc-7539-4837-935c-d1f3167fa8da","uuid":"07cd5a3c-5e15-4fea-a2b6-53178a688f4d","timestamp":"2026-10-13T05:29:22.994Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"8:## There was a second tree here until US-072\n18:## There were two trees here (US-052)\n67:## Rules every module follows\n112:## `cast.js` is the surface for work that has not happened (US-075)\n143:## `ipc-engine.js` is not an IPC module any more (US-044)\n177:  `null` endpoint is a wait rather than a failure -- the host already\n185:## Implementation pattern (set by `ipc-os.js`, US-020)\n222:## Gotchas\n  therefore has the host send the whole list as an array and the Tauri\n  shim spread it, so both trees hand the caller the same\n  `(first, ...rest)`. The parity test only checks arity, so a shim\n  that forgets to spread passes it and hands the caller one array.\n- The two **request/response exports** (`checkForExternalPlayer`,\n  `getEngineEndpoint`) were a `send` plus an `ipcRenderer.once` on the\n  same channel under Electron, which is why they read as the odd ones\n  out. Under Tauri they are ordinary `invoke` calls that resolve with\n  what the command returned -- the pair collapsed into the promise the\n  callers already had. Both still *wait on the host*, though, and\n  their commands are `async` for that reason (US-070, US-080): the\n  engine one waits out a sidecar restart, and the external-player one\n  probes every place VLC might be, a process each. A synchronous\n  command would do either on the main thread with the window frozen\n  behind it.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KQLx15YBaWVmdGe5eGqEoA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
